In this lesson we'll look at how sample size affects the reliability of your statistics. Here's the big idea: as your sample size grows, your sample mean or proportion homes in on the true population value, and as it shrinks, those sample statistics become way more jumpy and unpredictable. Picture this — if you take ten samples of just five people each, their average heights might scatter all over the place, from about 148 centimetres to 180. But bump that up to fifty people per sample, and those averages cluster tightly around the real value of 164 centimetres. Push it to five hundred, and the sample means are almost identical. Now here's a handy rule of thumb — doubling your sample size doesn't double your precision, it actually only divides the spread of your sample means by roughly the square root of two, so going from fifty to a hundred only halves the spread, not more. What does this mean for you? Bigger samples give you more precise estimates and narrower prediction intervals, while smaller samples are cheaper but their results can easily be off by pure chance. So before you collect any data, always ask yourself how precise you actually need to be, then pick a sample size big enough to deliver that. One quick warning though — a bigger sample fixes random variation, but it can't fix a biased sampling method, so make sure your sample is actually fair. Now let's see it in action.